          %h2#references References
          %div
            %p
              References on
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            %p#ReferencesCategoryTheoryHistory
            %h3#section_History History
            %p
              The concepts of
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              and
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
              were introduced in
            %ul
              %li#EilenbergMacLane45
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Samuel+Eilenberg"}> Samuel Eilenberg
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+MacLane"} Saunders MacLane
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/General+Theory+of+Natural+Equivalences"} General Theory of Natural Equivalences
                %strong 58
                2 (1945) 231-294 [
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1090/S0002-9947-1945-0013131-6"}> doi:10.1090/S0002-9947-1945-0013131-6
                ,
                = succeed "]" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.jstor.org/stable/1990284"} jstor:1990284
            %p
              apparently (see
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "Hans+Freudenthal#AsAForerunnerOfCategoryTheory"} there
            %ul
              %li#Freudenthal37
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hans+Freudenthal"}> Hans Freudenthal
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Entwicklungen von Räumen und ihren Gruppen
                %strong 4
                (1937) 145-234 [
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/CM_1937__4__145_0"}> numdam:CM_1937__4__145_0
                ]
            %p
              The rational for introducing the concept of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
              was to introduce the concept of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformations"} natural transformations
              (more specifically
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+equivalences"} natural equivalences
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
              and specifically in
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+objects"} dual objects
            %blockquote
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                = succeed ":]" do
                  %a{:href => "#Freyd64"} Freyd 64, page 1
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
                were publicly defined as the study of families of sets closed under finite intersection and infinite unions a serious disservice would be perpetrated on embryonic students of topology. The mathematical correctness of such a definition reveals nothing about topology except that its basic axioms can be made quite simple. And with category theory we are confronted with the same pedagogical problem. The basic axioms, which we will shortly be forced to give, are much too simple.
              %p A better (albeit not perfect) description of topology is that it is the study of continuous maps; and category theory is likewise better described as the theory of functors. Both descriptions are logically inadmissible as initial definitions, but they more accurately reflect both the present and the historical motivations of the subjects.
              %p#CategoriesForNaturalTransformations It is not too misleading, at least historically, to say that categories are what one must define in order to define natural transformations.
            %p and
            %blockquote
              %p#CategoriesForNaturalTransformationsII
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                = succeed "]:" do
                  %a{:href => "#Freyd65"} Freyd 65, beginning of Part Two
            %p
              The article by
              %a{:href => "#EilenbergMacLane45"} Eilenberg &amp; Maclane 1945
              was a clash of ideas from abstract
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
              (Mac Lane) and
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
              (Eilenberg). It was first rejected on the ground that it had no content. Since then category theory has flourished into almost all areas of mathematics, has found many applications outside mathematics and even attempts to build a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
              of mathematics.
            %p This and much more history is recalled in:
            %ul
              %li#MacLaneHistory
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+MacLane"}> Saunders MacLane
                  \:
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Concepts and Categories in Perspective
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em A Century of Mathematics in America, Part I
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "https://www.ams.org/publicoutreach/math-history/hmath1-index"} ISBN 0-8218-0124-4
                  %a{:href => "https://www.ams.org/publicoutreach/math-history/hmath1-maclane25.pdf"} pdf
                  %a ]
              %li{:id => "Krömer07"}
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ralf+Kr%C3%B6mer"}> Ralf Krömer
                  \:
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Tool and object: A history and philosophy of category theory
                  %strong 32
                  (2007) [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-7643-7524-9"}> doi:10.1007/978-3-7643-7524-9
                  ]
            %p see specifically
            %ul
              %li
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ralf+Kr%C3%B6mer"}> Ralf Krömer
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Category theory in Algebraic Topology
                %a{:href => "#Krömer07"} Krömer 2007
                [
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-7643-7524-9_2"}> doi:10.1007/978-3-7643-7524-9_2
                ]
            %p See also:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Pierre+Marquis"}> Jean-Pierre Marquis
                  \:
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em What is Category Theory?
